# vectorize-image

A tool that traces a two-tone (black/white) raster image — PNG, JPEG, etc. —
into an SVG made of cubic bezier curves, using a real curve-fitting algorithm
rather than just outlining pixels. It's a PySide6 desktop app for tracing an
image by hand, on layers (see [GUI](#gui) below).

## Install

```
python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e .
```

This installs the `vectorize-image` command.

## GUI

```
vectorize-image [optional/path/to/image.png]
```

(or `python -m vectorize_image.gui.app`)

Opening an image starts a fresh document of two layers: **Image**, holding
the picture, and **Drawing** above it — an empty vector drawing, and the
current layer, where the tools work.

**File → Import SVG** (Ctrl+I) adds an SVG file's shapes — paths, rects,
circles, ellipses, polygons, polylines and lines, with their transforms —
as a vector drawing in a new layer above the current one, named after the
file. Each filled shape becomes a polygon (its subpaths its rings), and an
unfilled one just its lines; colors, strokes, text, images and `<use>`
references aren't brought in. Like any layer change, it's one undo step.

### Layers

The **Layers** panel (a dock; show or hide it from **View → Layers**) lists
the layers top first. Each row has:

- a grip at its left end — **drag** it to move the layer up or down;
- an **eye** button that shows or hides the layer;
- the layer's name — **double-click** it to rename the layer (**Enter**
  renames, **Esc** or clicking away leaves it as it was);
- an **opacity** slider, 0–100%, for the whole layer;
- a **trash** button that deletes the layer.

The selected row is the current layer, which Pen draws in and Edit edits.
**+ New Layer** at the bottom adds an empty layer above the selected one.

### Tools

Two **Tool** buttons on the toolbar's second row:

#### Pen

A click-to-place "magnetic pen", drawing in the current layer:

1. **Click** to place a starting point — it snaps to the nearest edge in the
   image.
2. **Move the cursor**: a live single bezier curve previews from that point,
   snapped to and following the actual edge under the cursor.
3. **Click again** to commit that curve. Its end becomes the new starting
   point, and the process repeats for the next curve.
4. Click near an existing **open point** (orange — the end of a chain)
   instead of empty space, and the new curve connects to it rather than
   creating a new point. That's how a chain closes into a loop, or joins
   onto another chain — the highlighted point (cyan ring) shows what
   clicking now would connect to. A closed loop becomes a filled polygon;
   one closed inside another becomes a hole in it.
5. **Esc** stops the current chain. Its points stay, and clicking near its
   open end later continues right from there. A starting point that never
   got a curve leaves nothing behind.

The edges Pen snaps to are between the image's dark ink and its light
background, split at a threshold picked with Otsu's method.

#### Edit

Selects and changes what's in the current layer's drawing. Three toolbar
buttons pick what a click selects — each also has a number-key shortcut:

- **Edit points** (`1`) — a point: the end of a curve, or one of its
  control points (purple, with a dashed handle back to its end).
- **Edit lines** (`2`) — a whole curve, clicked anywhere along it.
- **Edit polygons** (`3`) — a whole polygon, clicked along its outline or
  anywhere inside it.

Three more pick *how* a click or drag builds the selection:

- **Move** (the default) — **click** an item to select just it;
  **Shift-click** another to add it to the selection, or a selected one to
  take it out. Clicking empty space clears the selection. **Drag** any
  selected item to move the whole selection together; dragging a point
  carries its control points along. The move is previewed while you drag,
  and is one step on the undo stack.
- **Box Select** — **drag a rectangle**; on release, everything it touches
  is selected (a curve or polygon only needs to pass through the box).
  Plain drag replaces the selection; Shift-drag adds to it.
- **Paint Select** — a circle follows the cursor; **press and drag** to select everything it
  touches as you sweep it around. Without Shift, a new stroke starts from
  an empty selection; with Shift, it adds to it.

**G** grabs the selection, as in Blender — whole drawings and images in
object mode too. It switches to Move, and what's selected (highlighted in
white) moves with the cursor straight away, no button held: **click** to
place it there, as one undo step, or **Esc** to put it back. While it's
moving, **X** or **Y** keeps it to that axis — shown by a line along it
(red for X, green for Y) through the middle of what's moving; pressing the
same key again frees it. (Clicking the Move button on the toolbar only switches tools.)

**Esc** cancels whatever's in progress (a move drag snaps back, a box is
abandoned, a paint stroke reverts), or else clears the selection.

**Edit → Delete Selection** (`Delete`/`Backspace`, also on the toolbar)
removes what's selected: points take the curves through them along; curves
leave their ends; polygons take their outline, except what a neighbouring
polygon shares.

**Edit → Flip Horizontally** (`H`) and **Flip Vertically** (`V`), also on
the toolbar, mirror the selection across the middle of the box around it,
as one undo step — in object mode whole drawings and images (an image's
pixels too), in edit mode points, curves or polygons. Selected points take
their control points along, so flipping a single point mirrors the curves'
tangents at it.

### Undo/redo

Every change to the document — a curve drawn, a move, a delete,
and adding, deleting, renaming or moving a layer — is one step
on an undo stack, via **Edit → Undo** / **Redo** (`Ctrl+Z` /
`Ctrl+Shift+Z`, also on the toolbar). The menu names the step, e.g.
"Undo Draw Curve".

### Display

Polygons are filled half-transparent red, over the image — and red is
what Export SVG fills them with. The current layer's curves and points
show while drawing or editing it, at a constant on-screen size at any zoom.

### Canvas navigation

Mouse and keyboard:

| Action | Mouse | Keyboard |
|---|---|---|
| Zoom in/out | Wheel — zooms on the point under the cursor, so that point stays put on screen | `+`/`-` (or `Ctrl+=`/`Ctrl+-`, or the toolbar/View menu) — zooms on the view's center |
| Pan | — (the left button belongs to Pen and Edit) | Arrow keys |
| Reset | — | **View → Fit to Window** (`Ctrl+0`) |

Zoom is clamped to a sane range (0.05x–40x) either way. Keyboard shortcuts
apply once the canvas has focus, which it grabs automatically after loading
an image.

Use **File → Export SVG** to export the polygons of every visible
layer.

## How it works

The pipeline has six stages (see `vectorize_image/pipeline.py`):

1. **Binarize** (`binarize.py`) — load the image, flatten transparency onto
   white, convert to grayscale, and threshold it into a black/white bitmap.
   If no `threshold` is given, the cutoff is chosen automatically with
   **Otsu's method** (maximizing between-class variance over the histogram).

2. **Trace** (`tracing.py`) — walk the grid of corners *between* pixels
   (the "crack" boundaries where the bitmap changes color) to extract every
   closed contour as a pixel-accurate, axis-aligned polygon. This is the
   same style of approach used by tools like potrace. Ambiguous
   checkerboard ("saddle") points are resolved by treating the foreground as
   4-connected, so diagonally-touching foreground pixels get separate loops
   rather than merging. Multiple contours (shapes, holes, holes-in-shapes,
   arbitrarily nested) fall out naturally as independent loops — orientation
   is never tracked, since each contour's fill role is resolved later by the
   `evenodd` fill rule rather than by winding direction.

3. **Simplify & find corners** (`simplify.py`) — reduce the staircase-shaped
   traced polygon with the **Douglas-Peucker algorithm**, then compute the
   turning angle at each remaining vertex. Vertices turning sharper than
   `corner_threshold_deg` are treated as true corners; the closed polygon is
   cut into open point-chains at those corners so each chain can be curve-fit
   independently, which preserves an honest cusp wherever chains meet. If a
   shape has no sharp corners at all (e.g. a circle), one seam is forced at
   the point of maximum curvature purely so the loop can be treated as an
   open chain — this doesn't introduce a visible corner in practice.

4. **Fit bezier curves** (`curve_fit.py`) — this is the core "bezier curve
   fitting algorithm": each point chain is fit with a sequence of cubic
   beziers using **Philip J. Schneider's curve-fitting algorithm**
   (*Graphics Gems*, 1990):
   - Parameterize points by cumulative chord length.
   - Fit one cubic bezier by least squares (holding the endpoints fixed and
     using estimated tangent directions, which reduces to a small 2x2 linear
     system for the two interior control points).
   - If the fit's max error exceeds `max_error`, refine the parameterization
     with a few rounds of Newton-Raphson root-finding and refit.
   - If it's still not good enough, split the chain at its worst point and
     recursively fit each half.

   The least-squares step is guarded against the classic failure mode of
   this algorithm: when a chain has very few interior points or
   near-parallel tangents, the linear system can become ill-conditioned and
   produce wildly oversized control points. Solutions outside a sane
   multiple of the chord length fall back to a simple heuristic instead.

5. **Group into shapes** (`simplify.compute_shape_roots`) — figure out which
   contours are nested inside which others (a hole inside an outer boundary,
   an island inside that hole, and so on) by containment, and group each
   such family under its top-level ancestor. This keeps a shape and its own
   holes together, while unrelated shapes elsewhere in the image end up in
   different groups.

6. **Emit SVG** (`svgwrite.py`) — each group becomes its own `<path
   id="shapeN">`, so independent shapes in the source image are independent,
   individually selectable/styleable elements in the output, rather than one
   big combined path. A shape's own holes stay inside its single path,
   rendered with `fill-rule="evenodd"` so they punch out correctly
   regardless of winding direction. Any bezier segment whose control points
   are (numerically) exactly on the chord is written as a plain line (`L`)
   instead of a curve (`C`) for a cleaner file.

## Limitations

- Designed for two-tone art (line drawings, logos, text, scanned documents),
  not photographic color images.
- The tracer is pure Python/NumPy; very large images (many megapixels) will
  be noticeably slower than a compiled tool like potrace. Downscale first if
  needed.
- Output is a single flat color — no color quantization or multi-layer
  tracing.

## Tests

```
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest
```
